A||N||17||Ditz88GT||Still overheating...but now dead, can anyone help?||||9||
Z||000000||Ditz88GT||06-02-2000||12:56 AM||||Here's the whole story.  About three months ago my Fiero (88 GT V6) started overheating.  It wasn't all the time.  It would shoot up out of nowhere, but not all the time.  It did this for about a week then started overheating very fast and all the time.  I took it to a radiator shop and they tested the cooling system and added some special chemical and determined it was a blown head gasket.  They said it was blowing the compression into the coolant.  So this week I pulled the motor apart and replaced the gaskets.  I started it up and it ran good...for about ten minutes.  The engine started getting hot AGAIN!  So I pulled over and left it cool off.  Now it won't start.  I think I have fuel, I can smell gas.  I checked to see if I had spark.  No such luck.  I first checked for spark at the plug, none.  I then checked the coil, once again none.  Is there some thing I'm missing?  Do you think it could be a bad coil?  Maybe the ignition mod or tach filter?  Even if I fix that and get it to fire does anyone have any ideas on the overheating problem?||||reg||9
Z||000001||Oreif||06-02-2000||02:33 AM||||If you have no spark it could be many things, Coil, pick-up coil inside distributor, or Ignition module.<BR>As for cooling, Was the cooling system filled properly? Filling a Fiero is a little different than a regular car. You need to remove radiator cap on front and fill from back until radiator overflows. Then cap radiator and top off the back. <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ditz88GT||06-02-2000||09:58 AM||||One more strange thing to add is that the radiator doesn't seem to get hot.  There is pressure but even the coolant doesn't seem to get hot.  I'm really confused.  Could I have a clog?  But I did fill it correctly.<p>[This message has been edited by Ditz88GT (edited 06-02-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||lennyb4||06-02-2000||10:59 AM||||I had a very simular problem about a month ago. Turned out to be the thermostat. It was opening BUT not enough. Try taking it completely out and see what happens then. If it works then just replace with a new one. Also might want to see if you fan is working properly.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lennyb4||06-02-2000||11:01 AM||||Oppss...Almost forgot. As for the loss of all power. GM modules have a bad history of popping when overheated. Certain autopart stores will test them for you at no charge.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ditz88GT||06-02-2000||11:42 AM||||Thanks for your help.  I did try pulling the Thermostat, and it still over heated.  I'm starting think I may have a clog.  If one of the pipes to the front is clogged and the other isn't, would it still build pressure up front?||||reg||5
Z||000006||lennyb4||06-02-2000||11:45 AM||||Yes. If the return pipe (i believe drivers side) is clogged it would still build pressure. Have you run the car with the thermo cap off to see if your getting flow? The best way to tell would be to add coolant die to the system. Do this via the front radiator fill cap to see if you are getting flow back to the engine (also makes it easy to spot any leaks or build up).<p>[This message has been edited by lennyb4 (edited 06-02-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ditz88GT||06-02-2000||12:08 PM||||This is great, finally I have some direction.  I'm feeling a little better about this.  Do you think the head gasket had anything to do with it.  I will try that test tomorrow.  Also I did run it without the cap and the water did move around.||||reg||7
Z||000008||prof bobo||06-02-2000||03:32 PM||||I just rebuilt a GM 4.3L for my Jimmy (I have a Fiero too, but that's another story . . .) It was overheating similar to what you described, radiator and hose next to thermostat cool.  Two thermostats later, same problem.  Turned out to be a bad catalytic converter.  Back pressure and heat from exhaust was superheating the coolant before it could circulate into the intake manifold and open the thermostat.  New converter, no overheating.||||reg||1
Z||000009||87PegasusGT||06-02-2000||03:47 PM||||Yup. I feel for you. Went through that problem described to a tee. It was blown head gasket as well. Your heater won't blow hot air either, will it? After new head gasket, it still had problems, but after a thorough bleeding, radiator, and low temp thermo, it will not go over 120.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ditz88GT||06-02-2000||07:54 PM||||Well, It's running!  I replace the Ignition module and it started right up.  I plan on flushing the system tomorrow.  I'm just not sure how to thoroghly bleed the system.  Do I open the front cap and keep filling until I don't see any air?||||reg||14
Z||000011||Ditz88GT||06-03-2000||06:57 PM||||Well, I flushed the the system and went for test drive.  Runs better than ever, but STILL OVERHEATS!  After it cooled down I looked under the car and it had kicking coolant out of the overflow.  I opened the cap at the engine, it was bone dry and the coolant up front was not real hot.  It looks to me like the coolant is getting to the radiator but not back.  Any Ideas???||||reg||5
Z||000012||Jim Baumann||06-04-2000||05:22 PM||||Did you make sure that the head was not warped before you reinstalled it?  On a different car that had overheated I had a intake manifold that had warped (was straight for a straight edge, had to take it in to be checked) and would not seal - kept leaking coolant into the oil no matter what  gasket/sealer mix I tried.  The head gasket is much more sensitive for warpage, the surfaces must align correctly.  You might still have a leak from the cylinder into the coolant.  Check your compression for starters.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ditz88GT||06-05-2000||11:55 AM||||Thanks Jim,  The compression looks good.  I'm starting to think that I have a major clog.  It seems like the coolant isn't getting back to the engine.  Correct me if I'm wrong (which happens a lot) the coolant flows from the engine up the Drivers side to the top of the radiator.  Then through the radiator and back down the passenger side and in the thermostat housing.  Is this correct?||||reg||5
Z||000014||Dan||06-05-2000||06:13 PM||||Sounds as perhaps you aren't filling properly.Be sure the coolant reservour is full after properly filling.Run to about 210 and turn engine off.One thing to look for too is air bubbles in the coolant.I have a ford v6 truck that had a warped head and thus a bad head gasket.It would blow air from the cylinder into  the coolant and would blow the coolant out of the radiator.Key is bubbles in the coolant while it is running.Machine shop charged me $35 to surface the heads.Properly fill.If it still overheats, check for bubbles.Let us know.||||reg||1
Z||000015||BAM-BAM||06-05-2000||07:21 PM||||    Read my post reply to fierostetz, that might help once you get it started again.<P> Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Ditz88GT||06-06-2000||02:23 PM||||Thanks Bam, does everyone have a different way of bleeding the cooling system?  I like your idea and I'll try it tomorrow night.  I think the water pump is good (I can see the water flow at front and back). If this doesn't work I think I may still have a leaky head or head gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Manta||06-07-2000||02:58 AM||||Just fixed my over heating problem. <BR>It also looked like it had good circulation.<BR>After 2 weeks of changing and flushing everything I gave up and took it to the radiator shop. It turnes out that the <BR>radiator was 90% clogged up. They rodded it and now the gauge wont go past 150, and I live in Nevada where it's been 110 every day.<BR>Your radiator shop might have overlooked this problem when they found the bad head gasket.||||reg||1
